WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.270
Film Courage: So Erik we’ve taken some questions
from viewers our community tab for Youtube.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.969
We have so many here and apologies to those
we don’t get to one of which is from Melissa

00:00:09.969 --> 00:00:15.139
Rose and Melissa writes “Is there one thing
you feel is essential for screenwriter/writer

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:16.760
to know before starting?”

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.050
Erik Bork, Screenwriter/Author: Well there
are a lot of things but I think the reason

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:27.320
I wrote this book about making the idea process
a priority is strictly based on y feeling

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:33.690
that you need to know that choosing the idea
that you’re going to write is so important

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:39.120
and so essential and not easy to pick and
idea that is going to be a worthwhile thing

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.510
to write.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.320
Worthwhile meaning it would have a chance
at moving it forward in the industry.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.610
It’s always worthwhile to write and have
the experience for yourself and for your growth.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:56.219
But fro a commercially marketable can move-my-career-forward
standpoint, to me the most important thing

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:59.690
which is why I wrote this book [The Idea:
The Seven Elements of a Viable Story for Screen,

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:04.059
Stage or Fiction] about is to understand what
the elements are of a viable story idea and

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:10.450
to make coming up with one your main priority
and take the time and get the feedback necessary

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:16.570
on your ideas before you launch into 6 months
or a year or several years writing and rewriting

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.140
a script because you can write or rewrite
a script several times to make it somewhat

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.150
better but if it was based on an idea that
it was really going to have a tough time moving

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.700
forward no matter how you executed it, a manager
or agent would call that somewhat wasted time.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:37.020
So that’s probably my first obvious, self-serving
answer based on my book being about that.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.650
Film Courage: Well that makes sense.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.000
Maybe we can actually go back to some of the
words in this P.R.O.B.L.E.M.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.000
acronym.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.399
So “Original,” we hear so much about “it’s
got to have this writer’s fresh voice”

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:54.800
and all of that but hasn’t so much already
been done?

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.150
It must be very challenging today to have
something “original.”

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:06.790
Erik: It is tough to be really original and
it goes back to writing things that are really

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.180
personal to you.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.130
Sometimes the most original things do come
from taking your real life or taking something

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.750
you have witnessed or experienced and using
that as fodder for material.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.000
So there can be a value for that if you can
find a way that we talked about which is find

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.370
a perspective so you can make it viable, entertaining
and compelling to millions of people who don’t

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:31.370
know you.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.110
But it’s not easy to do.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.060
Originality it’s a tricky one because you
can only be so original because so many things

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.620
have already been done right?

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.480
So many types of stories and genres and types
of story situations.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.720
I just saw EIGHTH GRADE last night which was
a very original voice which is something we’ve

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.660
already seen 4 million examples of which is
kind of a misfit teen who the popular people

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:01.860
are mean to trying to find their way in a
socially universe of school.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.710
Is there anything that has been done more
often than that?

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:10.700
But yet you watch the movie and because it’s
so authentic and real and specific and vivid

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.200
it was done not exactly in the way we’ve
seen it before and it had some currency because

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.590
there was the whole social media aspect to
it where she had like a vlog and she’s on

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.050
Instagram all the time it feels very much
like it’s about that problem for today’s

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.670
eighth graders in a certain setting.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.470
So you update it, you make it current and
you make it specific.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.810
I think it’s that the thing, not that it
has to be an idea that is so out there different

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:36.910
and special.

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.040
Because sometimes that can be a trap.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:45.981
If you try to be too original sometimes you
end up writing things that are contrived arguably

00:03:45.981 --> 00:03:49.890
or hard to believe or understand because your
focus is that is has to be different from

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:50.890
everything else.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:55.890
When a writer focuses too much on originality
(as I wrote in this book) sometimes they are

00:03:55.890 --> 00:04:00.780
doing it at the expense of these other important
elements of what makes a viable story idea.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.440
Like it may not be as compelling or entertaining
or as relatable because of these other things

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.660
because they are mainly focused on “Yeah,
but no one has ever seen it before.”

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:11.430
It doesn’t matter if no one has ever seen
it before if it doesn’t grab the audience

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.340
or the reader in the ways that great stories
always have.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:19.799
So you have to balance the need for originality
with these other elements.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.720
That being said you’re right that an original
voice is highly-valued in a new writer.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.120
It’s like finding that way to find an original
(I think authentic is a better word) but authenticity

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:36.830
where things feel so real, so believable but
so well-observed and unique and you haven’t

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:41.472
seen it quite this way before because something
about this writer’s way of seeing this way

00:04:41.472 --> 00:04:47.950
and the world came up with this version of
it that is very memorable even if it’s within

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:54.290
the context of something that is somewhat
familiar like if you look at NAPOLEON DYNAMITE

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:59.220
in its day kind of the same voice (kind of
the same thing) but it’s about an awkward

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.290
teen that doesn’t fit in and people push
him into lockers kind of thing.

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:08.600
But when you watch it you are mainly focused
on the authenticity and originality of it

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:15.320
not noticing that this is a story form or
a genre or a type of story problem that we’ve

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:16.690
seen in these variations.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:22.190
It’s kind of like you’re taking something
that has been done in terms of a genre but

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:26.220
you’re putting a very fresh, unique, current
spin on it.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:32.800
Film Courage: Right I’m thinking of PRETTY
AND PINK so for our generation that was sort

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.420
of the misfit movie (especially for a female
character).

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.520
Would this still work today?

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.170
Then you add in the vlogging element.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:46.200
I haven’t seen EIGHTH GRADE (it’s on my
list) but it’s sort of where she’s in

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:52.700
this town and she’s seeing the “soc”
group (I know that words not really used these

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.580
days) but sort of looking up and looking to
aspire.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.750
I think anybody can relate to that, it’s
a universal thing.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:05.880
But yeah you throw in the vlogging element
and the social media, that high school party

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.800
that everybody cringes at and you hope to
be noticed but they don’t really see you

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.310
and you blend into the background.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:21.080
Do you feel it’s still possible to do that
story over and over again?

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:27.230
I know you said they’ve added in these new
things the social media and the things that

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:29.400
are current today.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.370
That story is very universal whether it’s
male or female.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:37.870
Erik: Yes I do think it’s possible and Bo
Burnham proves it with that example that you

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.080
can do this thing we’ve seen so many times.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.670
You can instantly call up other movies that
have that same thing.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.389
I mean it has that high school party that
the character walks in and it petrified or

00:06:47.389 --> 00:06:49.500
doesn’t get noticed or gets noticed in a
negative way.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.100
It has that very scene but it has a unique
version of that scene that we’ve never seen

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:59.300
before that feels real and feels very well-observed
and is very entertaining and emotional to

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.300
watch.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.300
To me that’s the key.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.380
Don’t try to reinvent the wheel in terms
of genre or types of story problems but bring

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:13.370
something fresh, specific, real and original
to your treatment of that sort of genre element

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:18.130
that we know is universally relatable because
we know what a really hard thing that is to

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:23.419
know what you’re putting out is going to
be relatable to millions of people and the

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:32.480
kind of story situations that are going to
be a variety of them, there are certain core

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:36.610
elements that you tend to see repeated over
and over again in successful stories.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:40.360
That’s why I love [Author] Blake Snyder’s
10 genres in SAVE THE CAT, it’s one of my

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.340
favorite things about that book and I always
work with those 10 genres when I work with

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:50.449
writers writing screenplays because I think
he really pinpointed the kind of human situations

00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:54.930
that we’ve seen work over and over again
in successful movies and there are variations

00:07:54.930 --> 00:08:00.190
on each but there are certain key, core elements
that are present and sort of realize which

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:04.210
one you’re going for and then to really
try to fulfill those elements is a really

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.360
helpful tool for writers coming up with ideas
for films.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:13.570
Film Courage: So the last letter in your acronym
for P.R.O.B.L.E.M. is “meaningful.”

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:18.830
So taking EIGHTH GRADE or NAPOLEON DYNAMITE
or PRETTY IN PINK or whatever in that high

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:25.490
school not fitting in genre, what’s the
meaning that a movie like that could give?

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:28.610
You could just see it as Well hey everybody
had it tough in high school.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:32.589
It doesn’t matter if you were popular or
if you were bullied.

00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:34.789
Everybody had it tough to some degree.”

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:36.000
Where’s the meaning in that?

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.080
Erik: So I think if you walk away going “Everybody
had it tough, what’s the big deal?”

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.010
That movie didn’t affect you very much and
probably wasn’t meaningful to you right?

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:50.430
Meaningful to me is when we talk about theme
and what’s a movie really about underneath

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:51.950
its surface plot.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:57.940
What’s the message or what’s the underlying
issue being explored or issues and also what

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.370
the audience takes home with them.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.480
Do they feel impacted by that, do they feel
it has some meaning to their own lives as

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.510
opposed to I just watched these people have
this thing happen and I sort of forget about

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:08.510
it in the next moment.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:14.790
It sort of doesn’t really…nothing that
I saw impacts me and the view of my world

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:15.790
and people at all.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:19.770
It’s just a fictional story and when it’s
over, it’s very forgettable.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:26.900
So meaningful is the most optional of these
7 elements that I put in the book because

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.720
certain movies (let’s say TRANSFORMERS or
something) don’t necessarily need to be

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:35.500
so meaningful and have such a strong theme
like that.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.470
And I’m not dissing TRANSFORMERS and saying
that it doesn’t have any theme or meaning

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:44.100
but certain kind of movies if they’re so
entertaining and they have such a punishing

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.500
problem that is relatable enough to a universal
audience when you have life or death stakes

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.460
you usually have that one covered.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:57.190
Life altering takes, original and believable,
if it’s executed in ways that do all those

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:01.089
things, you could have a hit movie on your
hands even if it’s not that meaningful.

00:10:01.089 --> 00:10:04.760
But what most writers trying to break in are
trying to are do movies that have do a lot

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:05.760
of meaning.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.860
Because that’s what most writers are trying
to do is the theme and the character arc and

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:12.750
that kind of stuff that tends to give something
meaning.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:17.420
I also think when we talk about the originality
and the whole executing something in a brand

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:24.180
new way that feels very specific and well-observed
and real, that helps give it meaning to people

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.000
because if they feel like they are just watching
this contrived thing that is just there to

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:35.279
entertain them but nothing about it felt real
or my life or my kids or my memory of childhood

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:42.040
I went through that or it touches me, then
it’s not going to be meaningful to them.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:49.310
So meaningful is maybe the most optional and
the final one to consider but it can be the

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:54.000
most important one in the end that puts you
over the top because if your script makes

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.300
people really feel something and almost makes
them think about their own lives in some way

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:04.130
(even a hardened, professional reader in the
industry) if it really makes them feel like

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:09.710
I didn’t just watch these remote people
but I connected and it feels like it explored

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:13.270
something about the human condition that is
relevant to me and everyone, that’s really

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:13.271
powerful.

